<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Get Cool in the Shade – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Get Cool in the Shade.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b><br>
                Reading: Respond to texts with personal reflections.<br>
                Reading: Participate in group discussions based on reading assignments.<br>
                Phonemic Awareness: Decode English graphemes and phonemes using knowledge of word parts, syllabification, and phonemic awareness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b><br>
                Reading: Can respond to texts about conservation, with personal reflections (e.g., "I think saving water is important.").<br>
                Can participate in group discussions about conservation topics (e.g., "We can recycle more and turn off electronics when not in use.").<br>
                Can recognize and decode simple prefixes and suffixes in words (e.g., identifying "-re" in "recycle" and "-er" in "safer").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>
                (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of simple actions: "Turn off the light," "Save water," "Recycle," "Plant a tree." Students read the captions aloud chorally and then individually. The teacher asks students to point to pictures that help keep us "cool in the shade" or save "energy."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of a light switch and says, "Turn off the light." Then, points to a dripping faucet and says, "Let's save water." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of key words like "shade" /sh/ and "turn off" /t/. The teacher also models reading simple sentences with these phrases, emphasizing clear articulation and intonation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b><br>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If the sun is very hot, do we want to be in the 'shade' or in the sun?" (Expected answer: "Shade!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do we 'turn off' when we leave a room?" (Expected answer: "The lights!")</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher also reviews vocabulary from Lesson 1 by asking students to match words to pictures on the board (e.g., 'solar power' to a picture of solar panels, 'faucet' to a picture of a tap).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>
                (Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short, illustrated story or set of "Daily Conservation Tips" (genre: informational text/narrative, register: simple and encouraging, format: short paragraphs with pictures) titled "My Day to Save Energy!" The text describes simple conservation actions during different parts of the day: morning, noon, and night. It includes target vocabulary (e.g., "In the morning, I open the curtains for natural <b>light</b>," "At noon, we play in the <b>shade</b>," "At <b>night</b>, I <b>turn off</b> all the <b>electricity</b> and read a book. It's <b>easier</b> to relax in the dark!") and grammar (imperatives like "<b>Save water</b>!", comparatives like "It's <b>safer</b> to turn off lights when you leave.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading and understanding short, simple texts about familiar topics like daily routines and environmental conservation, specifically recognizing key information and simple suggestions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b><br>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> The teacher reads the text aloud once, then asks: "What is this story about? Is it about playing games or saving energy?" (Expected answer: "Saving energy!"). "When do we do these actions? Morning, noon, or night?" (Expected answer: "Morning, noon, and night!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read the text again silently. The teacher reads statements (e.g., "We turn off the lights in the morning." or "Playing in the shade is cool.") and students raise a 'True' or 'False' card.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match short sentences from the text (e.g., "Open curtains") to the correct time of day (morning, noon, or night) on a simple worksheet.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> As students read, the teacher points to words like "shade" and "turn off," asking students to identify the /sh/ and /t/ sounds. The teacher also highlights words like "recycle" and "safer," explaining that "-re" means "again" and "-er" often means "more." Students echo the words and sounds.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-17 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>
                (Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading sentences and phrases accurately, focusing on grammar (imperatives, comparatives, superlatives) and vocabulary (shade, light, water, energy, recycle, turn off, save, cool, easy, meaningful, bright, safe, easier, safer, most). Practicing decoding words with simple prefixes and suffixes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b><br>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled practice (Cloze Reading):</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from the "My Day to Save Energy!" text, but with key vocabulary words missing. They read each sentence and choose the correct word from a word bank (e.g., "Let's _____ water." (save/waste); "Use natural _____." (light/dark)). Teacher circulates to check understanding and pronunciation of the words, especially those with target phonemes and word parts (e.g., 'recycle').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled practice (Sentence Scramble):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives strips of paper with jumbled words that form sentences from the text, including imperatives and comparatives (e.g., "lights / Turn off / the / when / not / use / in"). Students read the words and arrange them to form correct sentences. They then read their completed sentences aloud to their partner.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Reading and Identifying):</b> Students stay in pairs. They receive a list of "good" and "bad" conservation actions. They read each action (e.g., "Leave the faucet dripping." "Switch off unused bulbs.") and mark it as "Good" or "Bad." They then read the 'Good' actions to their partner, using imperatives like "Let's save energy!" or "Recycle more!" They also identify words with the prefixes/suffixes discussed.</li>
                </ol></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>
                (Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Discussion based on Reading.<br>
                <b>Connect to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher provides each group with a short new text titled "Our School's Energy Challenge." This text lists 3-4 different conservation actions (e.g., "Using solar power is good.", "Turning off computers saves electricity.", "Recycling paper is important."). Each action is described with a simple sentence. Students read the text individually first. Then, in their groups, they discuss the actions they have read. They must identify "the most important" action from the text and explain why, responding to the text with personal reflections. They should use comparatives (e.g., "Saving electricity is <b>easier than</b> changing all the bulbs.") and superlatives (e.g., "I think recycling paper is <b>the most important</b>."). They also practice giving suggested actions ("We can <b>turn off</b> the electronics when not in use."). Each group prepares to share their "most important" action and reason with the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in reading and expressing simple opinions, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion (agreeing on the most important action). The focus is on *reading* the challenge and *discussing* their understanding and reflections based on that reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>
                (Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussions and individual reading responses.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their group's chosen "most important" conservation action and their reason to the class. The teacher observes their ability to read and articulate their reflections and participate in the group discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b><br>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Reading Comprehension:</b> Can identify key information in the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Incorporates words like "save," "energy," "recycle," "environment," "important," "easier," "most."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Grammar:</b> Uses imperatives (e.g., "Let's recycle!") and comparatives/superlatives (e.g., "the most important," "easier than").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Personal Reflection:</b> Expresses a simple opinion or reflection based on the text (e.g., "I think saving water is important.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> Attempts to decode words with introduced prefixes/suffixes (e.g., "recycle," "safer").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Participates in group discussion based on reading assignments.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students receive a short paper with two prompts to complete before leaving class:<br>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new thing I learned about saving energy from reading today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Reading about conservation makes me feel..." (e.g., happy, thoughtful, ready to help).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-6 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b><br>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's reading activity was the most interesting for you?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you can do at home this 'morning, noon, or night' to help conserve energy?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b><br>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The reading activities generally worked well, especially the comprehension tasks and the cloze reading. Students were able to identify key vocabulary and understand simple imperatives. The group discussion for production required a bit more scaffolding for some groups to confidently use comparatives and superlatives, but the overall engagement with the text and topic was good. I need to ensure more explicit modeling of expressing preferences (e.g., "I prefer saving water...") in the next lesson. Some students struggled slightly with decoding words containing the new suffixes, so integrating a brief, focused activity on word parts will be beneficial in future lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> For Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will build on their reading comprehension by actively speaking and interacting in pairs, using the conservation vocabulary and grammar (imperatives, comparatives, superlatives) to discuss and recommend actions for saving energy and water in different scenarios. They will use the ideas and reflections from today's reading to formulate their spoken responses.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>